text:
maria: Anyone hitting weird partial-JSON errors when consuming SSE from Node SDK? long assistant responses get split and JSON.parse throws.
ben: Yeah, saw that. Happens when chunk boundary splits inside a string or object. Parser expects whole object.
kai: Workaround: incremental buffer and try parse when you see a full event delimiter. We use `buffer += chunk` then loop `while (buffer.indexOf('\n\n') !== -1)` to extract events.
sloane: Here's a tiny snippet we've been using:
```
let buf = '';
stream.on('data', c => {
  buf += c.toString();
  let parts = buf.split('\n\n');
  buf = parts.pop();
  for (const p of parts) {
    if (!p.trim()) continue;
    // strip leading "data: " lines
    const obj = p.split('\n').map(l => l.replace(/^data:\s*/, '')).join('\n');
    try { handle(JSON.parse(obj)); } catch (e) { console.warn('partial json, requeue', e); }
  }
});
```
ben: That helps but also watch auth refresh. If token refresh triggers reconnect mid-response you get a truncated last event.
maria: Good call — we saw 401 mid-stream due to token expiry. We switched to long-lived stream token + periodic heartbeat.
stream-bot: deploy: staging rollout of sse-parser-fix succeeded :rocket:
kai: For backpressure, pause the incoming socket while your queue length > N, then resume. In Node: `req.pause()` / `req.resume()`.
sloane: Python folks — use aiohttp's client.content.iter_chunked and buffer similarly. Also consider an incremental JSON parser (ijson) for huge objects.
maria: TL;DR: buffer until SSE delimiter, handle token refresh before reconnect, and implement pause/resume on surge. Anyone want to pair for a quick PR review tomorrow? @ben @kai
